<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antiochus VII Sidetes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 129 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antiochus VII Sidetes was a Seleucid king who attempted to restore the dynasty's power in the second century BCE. He launched a major campaign to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> but was defeated and killed in 129 BCE. His death ended Seleucid hopes of regaining the eastern provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-vii-sidetes.jpg" alt="Antiochus VII Sidetes" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Antiochus_VII_Euergetes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antiochus VII Euergetes, nicknamed Sidetes after the city of Side in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and also known as Antiochus the Pious, was born around 164 to 160 BCE. He was a son of Demetrius I Soter and the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II Nicator</a>; his mother may have been Laodice V. He was raised to the throne after Demetrius II was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, and he married Cleopatra Thea, who had been Demetrius's wife. Their son became Antiochus IX.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Antiochus ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> from July or August 138 to 129 BCE and spent his nine years trying to undo the heavy territorial and authority losses of recent decades. He defeated the usurper Diodotus Tryphon at Dora and in 134 BCE laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. During the siege he allowed a seven-day truce so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> could celebrate a religious festival, which impressed their leaders.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, the Hasmonean leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> opened the tomb of King David, removed three thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and paid Antiochus to spare the city. His respectful treatment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> earned him their gratitude and the added name Euergetes, the Benefactor. Because no Jewish sources of the time survive, it is unclear whether the siege ended in a decisive Seleucid victory or a peace treaty. Jewish forces later assisted his wars, and for nearly twenty years after his death John Hyrcanus refrained from attacking Seleucid territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years Antiochus tried to reclaim the eastern territories overrun by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I</a>. He marched east with what proved to be the last great Seleucid royal army, including a unit of Judean troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a>, and won three battles. He restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and Media before dispersing his army into winter quarters.</p><p>Tension grew between the local population and the troops quartered upon them. The new Parthian ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phraates-ii">Phraates II</a>, released his prisoner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II</a>, who returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to reclaim the throne. In the winter of 130–129 BCE several towns rose against their Seleucid garrisons, and Antiochus marched to relieve them. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> mountains he was ambushed and killed by Phraates II and a large Parthian force. Afterwards the Seleucid realm was restricted to Syria. His confirmed heir was Antiochus IX Cyzicenus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antiochus VII Sidetes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Antiochus VII Sidetes</b> made the last serious attempt to reverse these losses, briefly reconquering Babylonia before his death in battle against Phraates II in 129 BCE, after which no Seleucid ruler had the resources to contest Parthian control of the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Side, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.77,31.39&amp;year=-139" aria-label="Side on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5338 839 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antiochus VII Sidetes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 1109h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 1109h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.77,31.39&amp;year=-139">What was here in 139 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
